This suite checks how an MDS rank recovers when ganesha's libcephfs client
misses the reconnect window after the rank fails over.

Ganesha (FSAL_CEPH) sets a session timeout on its libcephfs client, which
the MDS sees as client metadata "timeout". At reconnect timeout, the MDS
evicts clients that did not reconnect, but keeps such a session ("reconnect
keeps ..., need to be reclaimed") and holds the rank in up:clientreplay
until the session is gone. That happens when a new ganesha instance
reclaims it (by the same uuid, when it starts), when an admin evicts it,
or when it is autoclosed after session_autoclose seconds without a cap
renewal. All the other clients of the rank wait meanwhile.

Each job runs one scenario, picked by scenario/. The ganesha-reclaim task
freezes ganesha (SIGSTOP) and fails rank 0, checks that the new MDS holds
the rank with ganesha's session kept, then:

  restart   kills and restarts ganesha; the new instance has to reclaim
            the session and the rank has to go active
  evict     evicts the session; the rank has to go active
  late      resumes ganesha, which missed the reconnect but is still
            running, and writes a file over NFS; the rank has to go
            active and the write has to complete

The late job fails: the rank is held until ganesha's session is
autoclosed. Ganesha's client does not reconnect to the new MDS. Stopped
for longer than mon_client_ping_timeout, it reopens its mon session when
it resumes, which drops the mdsmaps still queued on the old one, and the
mon sends it only the current map. So it never sees the rank in
up:reconnect and closes its side of the session ("we may miss the
MDSMap::RECONNECT" in its client log). Nor does it open a new session
while the rank is not active: its requests wait for an mdsmap with the
rank active. Nothing renews the kept session, so it is
autoclosed session_autoclose seconds after the reconnect started
("evicting unresponsive client ..., after 300 seconds"). Only then does
the rank go active, and as ganesha's client is blocklisted by the
eviction, the NFS write fails (EREMOTEIO). The task reports how long the
rank was held and whether ganesha's session was evicted.

Without the timeout metadata (kernel client, ceph-fuse), a client that
misses the reconnect is evicted at reconnect timeout and does not hold the
rank.
